==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|futtek}}, {{m|enm|votek}}, of uncertain origin; perhaps a compound of {{inh|en|ang|fōt}} + {{m|en|ang|hōc}}, or {{compound|enm|fut|-uc|nocat=1}} ({{m|en|foot|}} {{m|en|hook}} or {{m|en|foot|}} {{m|en|-ock}}), {{der|en|odt|vot}} or {{m|odt|fuot}} + {{m|odt|hoek}}; or from {{m|enm|timber}} {{m|enm|jargon}} word {{suffix|en|butt|ock|pos2=diminutive suffix}}. Alternatively from {{der|en|dum|voetkijn}}, diminutive of {{m|dum|voet|t=foot}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|foot|kin|nocat=1}}; or {{inh|en|ang|*fōtuc}}, meaning {{suffix|en|foot|ock|pos2=diminutive suffix}}.

Perhaps came into Old English from {{der|en|non|fótr}}, or {{m|non|fett}} / {{m|non|futt}} ({{m|en|big}}); + {{m|sv|ek}} ({{m|non|timbr}}), or {{m|non|øks}}; giving {{der|en|non|fót'ek}}, {{m|non|futtek}} or {{m|non|futtøks}}; equivalent {{m|en|Norwegian}} is {{m|non|fot}} {{m|non|haki}}, {{m|non|fett}} {{m|non|eik}} ({{m|non|tømmer}}), or {{m|non|fett}} {{m|non|øks}}; meaning {{m|en|foot}} {{m|en|hook}}, {{m|en|big}} {{m|en|oak}} ({{m|en|timber}}), or {{m|en|bold}} {{m|en|axe}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈfʌtək/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-futtock.wav|Audio (UK)}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈfʌtək/|[ˈfəɾək]}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|nautical}} Any of the [[curved]] [[rib]]-like [[timber]]s that form the [[frame]] of a wooden [[ship]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1884|author={{w|Dixon Kemp}}|title=A Manual of Yacht and Boat Sailing (Fourth Edition)|page=12|passage=The timbers (called also frames, or floors, first, second and third '''futtocks''', where the lengths of the frames are in two, three, or more pieces) will be "double," that is, two timbers will be placed close together, or nearly close together, and act as one frame.}}

